    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Pataliputra grew from the village of Pataligrama into the capital of the Nanda, Maurya and Gupta empires, and at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, Gandhaka and Son rivers it dominated the river trade of the Indo-Gangetic Plain.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> in the 3rd century BCE it was one of the largest cities in the world, with a wooden wall and 64 gates, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a>, a Greek ambassador, lived there and described it.</li><li>A shifting river, a decline in trade, a flood and perhaps an invasion ended its greatness, and by 637 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> found a ruined city with a small walled town of about 1,000 people; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a> later made the place his capital as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a> visited about 400 CE, he found that the city's nobles and householders had built hospitals where the poor, the crippled and the diseased of all countries received food, drink and medicines free of charge.</p></aside>
    </section>

