    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">490 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a> builds a fort at the village of Pataligrama, the beginning of the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 320 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> begins, and Pataliputra is its capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">303 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> mentions Pataliputra in his Indika</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">3rd century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> the city holds about 150,000 to 400,000 people and a Buddhist council meets there</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 400</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a> visits the Gupta capital and finds the people rich and prosperous</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">late 500s</span><span class="rf-text">A flood on the Son River likely devastates the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">637</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> finds the old city in ruins and a small walled town on the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">16th century</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a> makes the site his capital and renames it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ajatashatru.jpg" alt="Ajatashatru" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_Museum_Sculpture_-_Ajatasattu_worships_the_Buddha_(9217704485).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Dharma from Penang, Malaysia, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pataliputra stood at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, Gandhaka and Son rivers, a position that made it a water fort, or jaldurga. The city was formed by fortifying a village, Pataligrama, which is tied to the Haryanka ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a>, who built a small fort there near the Ganges in 490 BCE. Another account says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udayin">Udayin</a> laid the foundation of the city at the confluence of the Son and the Ganges and moved his capital from Rajgriha because of its central location, and Jain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> sources name Udayabhadra, a son of Ajatashatru, as the king who first made it the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>.</p><p>The written sources are late. Pataliputra does not appear before the early Jain and Buddhist texts, where it is the village of Pataligrama and is left out of a list of the major cities of the region. Early Buddhist sources report a city being built near the village toward the end of the Buddha's life, and this generally agrees with archaeological evidence of urban development in the area no earlier than the 3rd or 4th century BCE. The name has a traditional etymology from the patali tree, a plant whose seedpods break open, according to the Mahaparinibbana Sutta; another tradition makes it the son of Patali, daughter of a Raja Sudarsan. It was also called Kusumapura, the city of flowers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashoka.jpg" alt="Ashoka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka%27s_visit_to_the_Ramagrama_stupa_Sanchi_Stupa_1_Southern_gateway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Dharma from Sadao, Thailand, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The central position of Pataliputra in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> led successive dynasties to base their administrative capital there. Its place on the rivers let it dominate the riverine trade of the Indo-Gangetic Plain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>'s early imperial period, and it was a great centre of trade and commerce that drew merchants and intellectuals from all over India, among them the famed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Chanakya</a>.</p><p>The city reached its pinnacle of prosperity as the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>. In the 3rd century BCE, in Ashoka's reign, it was one of the largest cities in the world, with a population of about 150,000 to 400,000. It was estimated to cover 25.5 square kilometres, with a circumference of 33.8 kilometres, in the shape of a parallelogram, with 64 gates. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a>, who resided there as a Greek ambassador, called it Palibothra and left a detailed account of it. He wrote that the city ran ten miles in length and one and three quarter miles in breadth, with a ditch 600 feet wide and 45 feet deep, and a wall with 570 towers and 64 gates. Under Ashoka the wooden structures of the palace may gradually have been replaced by stone, and the palace shows decorative influences from the Achaemenid palaces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, possibly through craftsmen from abroad.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pataliputra was the capital of the Shishunaga Empire (c. 413 to 345 BCE) and of the Nanda Empire (c. 460 or 420 to c. 325 BCE). A Sangam Tamil epic, the Akananuru, mentions Nanda kings ruling there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> (c. 320 to 180 BCE) made it a capital in turn, and the city is said to have reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>.</p><p>The city was also the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> (c. 320 to 550 CE). The Shungas were among the dynasties that based their administrative capital there, and the Pala dynasty of the 8th to 12th centuries was another, though archaeologists have found no Pala period evidence at Pataliputra. In the early 800s, according to the Khalimpur plate of Dharmapala, Pataliputra was a river port and royal encampment where the kings of Jambudvipa assembled to pay homage to Dharmapala. B. P. Sinha read the inscription to mean that Pataliputra was Dharmapala's capital, but A. S. Altekar disputed this, holding that the inscription refers to it only as a skandhavara, a camp where he stayed during a campaign or tour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a>, in the 16th century, made the place his capital and changed the name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy.jpg" alt="Geography (Ptolemy)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy">Geography (Ptolemy)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Servet_Ptolomei_geographicae_enarrationis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Miguel Servet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> credited the Mauryan city with one of the first highly efficient forms of local self-government in the world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy">Geographia</a>, described the city as a parallelogram with a wooden wall pierced with openings through which arrows might be shot, and a ditch in front that served for defence and as a sewer. The wooden palisades found in the excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> are thought to be these walls. Archaeological research has found portions of the palisade over several kilometres, but no stone fortifications. Aelian wrote that the palaces of the Indian kings surpassed in splendour those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>, and that the parks held tame peacocks and pheasants.</p><p>Pataliputra was a Buddhist centre with a number of important monasteries, among them the Kukkutarama on the south-east side of the city. Early Buddhist texts record two important councils held there: the second session of the Second Buddhist council, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhist-councils">Third Buddhist council</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s reign. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a> visited about 400 CE, he found the people rich and prosperous, practising virtue and justice. The nobles and householders had built hospitals where the poor of all countries, the destitute, the crippled and the diseased received treatment and every kind of help free of charge, and physicians inspected the diseases and ordered food, drink and medicines.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xuanzang.jpg" alt="Xuanzang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xuanzang_w.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The decline of Pataliputra probably began well before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a>. At Kumrahar the archaeological evidence points to a gradual decline beginning in the 300s, with fewer and less elaborate structures between then and about 600, and after that there are no traces of human activity for a thousand years, and the site seems to have been abandoned. Several causes are proposed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a> wrote around 400 that the city was one yojana, about 10 kilometres, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, and the Varaha Purana, from after the Gupta period, indicates that the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandaki-river">Gandak</a> and the Ganges was then about 10 kilometres north of its present place. A city that had drawn much of its prosperity from river commerce was probably hurt by being cut off from the river, and a general decline in international trade at the end of the Gupta period would have had a similar effect.</p><p>A flood is also thought to have devastated the city at some point in the late 500s. The later Jain work Titlhogali Painniya records a traditional account of a disastrous flood on the Son River, in the month of Bhadrapada, after 17 days and nights of heavy rain. The Ganges is said to have overflowed as well, and Pataliputra was flooded on several sides, though the account says the city was rebuilt. A third possible cause is deliberate destruction by invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huna-people">Hunas</a> in the early 500s. A thick layer of ashes in the 80-pillar hall at Kumrahar suggests that the building may have burnt down. When Xuanzang came in 637 he found the city in ruins, the old city deserted for many years, and only a small walled town by the Ganges holding no more than about 1,000 people. According to Rajeshwar Prasad Singh, that town was probably built after the old city's destruction.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Pataliputra&#8221; cites where it covers Decline:</p><ul><li>B. P. Sinha, Pāṭaliputra Excavation, 1955-56 (Directorate of Archaeology and Museums, Bihar, 1970)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> found most of the old historic buildings destroyed, with only their foundation walls left. He singled out an old stupa that was said to be the first of the 84,000 stupas built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>; its foundation had sunk into the ground so that only the ornamental top of the dome was visible, and that in a precarious state. Pataliputra later appears in distant sources: in 1559 the Italian Caius Julius Solinus briefly mentions in a fanciful book of world geography a powerful Indian kingdom of Prasia with its capital at Palibotra.</p><p>The location of the site was first identified in modern times in 1892 by Laurence Waddell, in Discovery of the Exact Site of Asoka's Classic Capital. Extensive excavations have been made near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>. Early in the 20th century they revealed large fortification walls with reinforcing wooden trusses. Parts of the ancient city have been excavated at Kumhrar, Bulandi Bagh and Agam Kuan, but much of it still lies buried beneath modern Patna. Ashoka's Major Rock Edict No. 5 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girnar">Girnar</a> names Pataliputra, and it is recorded in the Buddhist and Jain texts and in the Greek accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Pataliputra, beside modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, grew from the village of Pataligrama into the capital of the Nanda, Maurya and Gupta empires and was one of the largest cities in the world under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>. It declined from the 300s, lay in ruins by 637, and later became Patna.</em></p>

